WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.312 --> 00:00:08.890
I love him.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:09.807
But I'm telling you...

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.370
So Tim - sourdough - very
fashionable, everyone loves it.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.050
What makes a perfect
starter, and what's

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.595
the best way to look
after it and get it going?

00:00:20.595 --> 00:00:22.890
Well, you start off with
some flour, preferably,

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.080
if you can get it, some
sort of unbleached flour,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.910
so it's still got some
natural yeast in it.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.855
You add an equal amount
of water to that,

00:00:29.855 --> 00:00:31.230
and you leave it
standing around.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.910
You can speed that
process up by putting

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.920
in a handful of
dried fruit, and it

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.660
will collect the
yeasts in the air

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.232
and start a sourdough
culture moving.

00:00:38.232 --> 00:00:39.690
And after a couple
of days, it will

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.930
start to bubble a little bit,
and that's your starting point.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.960
You feed it with an equal
amount of flour and water.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.380
Each time I want to feed
it, I pour away most of it.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.050
50 to 100 grammes
left in the bottom.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.110
And then to that I add
roughly 200 grammes of flour,

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.165
200 grammes of water
every time I feed it.

00:00:57.165 --> 00:00:59.290
And it's one of those things
that works really well

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.425
if you've been doing
it all your life.

00:01:01.425 --> 00:01:03.000
Which I haven't been doing.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.950
I've had mixed results
with my starters.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.670
There's this one, which was
last fed about two days ago,

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.995
I think, three days ago which
I almost poured on my laptop.

00:01:11.995 --> 00:01:13.620
In fact, I did just
pour on the laptop.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.345
That's a good start.

00:01:15.345 --> 00:01:17.220
That's the one that's
been fed more recently.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.470
It's actually been bubbling
over like a magic potion.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:20.940
The simplest thing that we
could do with it, I thought,

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:21.850
would be pizzas.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.350
Takes away all the
fuss about trying

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.160
to shape the loaf,
which is so complicated.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.280
So get yourself a bowl.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.080
I'm going to use a mixture of
strong flour and plain flour.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.260
They've run out everywhere.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.750
So I went straight
to the bakery.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.773
They only sell it
in 16-kilo sacks.

00:01:35.773 --> 00:01:38.190
So I've been distributing to
all of my friends and family,

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:38.930
anyone who needs it.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.097
How much did you pay for it?

00:01:40.097 --> 00:01:41.520
£15 for 16 kilos.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.652
So that is a really, really
decent, generous baker

00:01:43.652 --> 00:01:44.360
you've got there.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:45.930
If he tries to sell
it to you for 60 quid

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.138
You probably could sell it
for 60 quid at the moment.

00:01:48.138 --> 00:01:51.420
So we'll put in 150 grammes
of strong flour, 150 of plain,

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.120
10 grammes of salt, 50
grammes of olive oil.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.940
Pour in 200 grammes
of your starter.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.875
You say "pour."

00:01:58.875 --> 00:02:00.250
I wonder where
the spoon might...

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:00.990
Spoon is fine.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.510
Well, it's like looking
alive, which of course, it is.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.580
So what should
that take it up to?

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.630
My maths is terrible.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:07.702
Zero it every time.

00:02:07.702 --> 00:02:08.910
Oh, God, I haven't done that.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.160
So you'd have been on 360.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.100
OK, so I need to
get it up to 460?

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:14.965
560.

00:02:14.965 --> 00:02:16.590
And the last thing
you're going to need

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.675
is going to be 150
grammes of water.

00:02:18.675 --> 00:02:22.265
Zero it every time, of
course you should do that.

00:02:22.265 --> 00:02:23.730
I love him, but
I'm telling you...

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.810
Use a fork, or
something like that,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.510
to just really, really
loosely mix that together.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.460
And then let it sit
for about half an hour.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.880
Just knead it until
it's good and elastic,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.537
and we can come
back to it later.

00:02:37.537 --> 00:02:38.620
Take your clove of garlic.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.447
Cut it across the middle.

00:02:40.447 --> 00:02:42.030
Empty the garlicky
bits out of the top

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.488
and put them straight into the
bottom of your roasting tin.

00:02:44.488 --> 00:02:46.080
Pour in two cans
of tomatoes on top.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.050
You add pepper and salt to that,
and a squidge of olive oil.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.235
Just give it a
really loose stir.

00:02:51.235 --> 00:02:53.625
My granddad used to always
put sugar in his tomato sauce.

00:02:53.625 --> 00:02:54.750
Where do you stand on that?

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.458
You shouldn't need it
with decent tomato.

00:02:56.458 --> 00:02:57.795
His tomato sauce was great.

00:02:57.795 --> 00:03:00.023
What you can do is a
shot of balsamic vinegar.

00:03:00.023 --> 00:03:01.440
It also adds a
little bit of sour.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:02.955
So then just chuck
the lot in the oven.

00:03:02.955 --> 00:03:03.680
That's so simple.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.460
I don't think I'm never going to
make a tomato sauce on the hob

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:05.960
again.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.430
That's it.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.320
Mine looks a little
black around the edges.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.280
Should I be worried about that?

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:11.780
That's fine.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:12.605
Scrape it in.

00:03:12.605 --> 00:03:13.105
Yeah.

00:03:13.105 --> 00:03:14.860
All of your garlic should
have gone completely flat,

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.235
so you should be able to do
this by mashing with a spoon.

00:03:17.235 --> 00:03:21.000
This sauce is the absolute
New York pizzeria.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.680
It's like an American standard
because you can do it really,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.550
really quickly by putting the
sauce ingredients in the pizza

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.460
oven.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:31.320
It's a good, old school sort
of catering industry trick.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.010
That's it.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.010
That's the sauce.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.540
All of our sourdough
cultures are different,

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:35.873
so they work at different rates.

00:03:35.873 --> 00:03:39.870
So the rule that we tend to
use is, has it doubled in size?

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.880
Now once yours has doubled
in size, it's done its thing.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.030
And then you need to
cut it into four pieces

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.058
and form each of those
pieces into a neat ball.

00:03:47.058 --> 00:03:48.600
You don't have to
worry about keeping

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.223
everything tight and neat
doing the shaping bit.

00:03:51.223 --> 00:03:52.640
Just make them
roughly into balls,

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:54.360
and then stack them
up in a tray and put

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:55.590
that tray in the fridge.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.420
In the industry, we
call this retarding.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.690
We're going to retard
the dough overnight,

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.857
and that's going to give
it a time to develop better

00:04:01.857 --> 00:04:03.450
flavours.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.107
Put some flour on the table.

00:04:05.107 --> 00:04:06.190
Pick up one of your balls.

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:07.690
Place it in the
middle of the flour.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:11.970
Allow it to use its own
weight to kind of stretch out,

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.170
by passing it from
finger to finger.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.980
And as soon as you start to
get a sort of round shape,

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.510
put it down on the flour.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.626
Pull it out sideways
with your fingers.

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:21.209
You've got it.

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:21.570
That's good.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:22.190
That's really good.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:23.690
It doesn't take
much more than that.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:25.080
Now if you were
an Italian, you'd

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.940
now throw IT up in
the air and catch it.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.520
If you get a tear in it,
you can squeeze it close.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:30.630
Have you got a peel?

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:31.200
A peel?

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.700
One of those things for shooting
a pizza onto a pizza stone.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.002
Oh, yeah, a big shovel thing?

00:04:35.002 --> 00:04:36.210
Go get your big shovel thing.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.755
It's way too big
for our kitchen.

00:04:37.755 --> 00:04:38.880
It's absolutely ridiculous.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:39.700
Wait until you see this.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:40.242
It's perfect.

00:04:40.242 --> 00:04:41.333
That's a commercial one.

00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:42.310
Yes.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.448
I'm using a circular tin tray.

00:04:44.448 --> 00:04:46.740
Put some flour on the peel
and then put the pizza dough

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:47.423
on top of it.

00:04:47.423 --> 00:04:48.840
And then sort of
shuggle it about,

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.630
just to make sure that
it moves on the peel.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:54.420
So I've got a pizza stone,
which, of course, heats

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.520
the pizza up to a much
higher temperature

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.140
than my oven would do otherwise.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.060
Can you do these kind of
pizzas just in a regular oven?

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:01.560
Totally.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.438
The thinner the
steel tray you use,

00:05:03.438 --> 00:05:04.980
if you're putting
it on an oven tray,

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:06.716
the better, because that
will heat through quicker.

00:05:06.716 --> 00:05:07.216
Oh, OK.

00:05:07.216 --> 00:05:10.130
Continuing to stretch it just a
little bit around the outside,

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:12.372
leaving a slightly raised
crust, and now mine's

00:05:12.372 --> 00:05:14.330
perfectly circular, and
I'm very happy with it.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.172
Don't put heavy
downward pressure on it.

00:05:17.172 --> 00:05:19.130
Because that way you'll
probably tear the base,

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:20.213
which we don't want to do.

00:05:20.213 --> 00:05:22.790
Use a maximum of two
tablespoons full.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.620
Now sprinkle of
black pepper on top.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:26.010
Cheap stick-block mozzarella.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.010
That seems to be difficult
to get at the moment,

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:29.750
so I got this off-brand burrata.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.623
Tear it into little pieces
and drop them quite carefully.

00:05:32.623 --> 00:05:35.040
I've got some particularly
fine olive oil floating about -

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.470
a little squirt of
that on top of it.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.660
A fresh basil leaf can go
on top of there, as well.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:39.952
Let's give it a little shuggle.

00:05:39.952 --> 00:05:40.610
It's moving.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:43.160
Do not take it to the oven
until you're absolutely

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.940
positive you can shoot it off.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:45.440
Ah!

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.005
It almost slid off the shovel.

00:05:47.005 --> 00:05:49.100
OK, let's put them in the oven.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.135
Ah, there she goes.

00:05:51.135 --> 00:05:53.510
Yeah, that shouldn't take too
long, right - five minutes,

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:54.170
or something?

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:54.950
If that.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:56.930
Tim, where do you stand
on pizza toppings?

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:58.850
I always got the
sense that the purists

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:01.930
think that just
cheese and tomato is

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.315
how you judge whether a pizza
is a really good quality.

00:06:04.315 --> 00:06:05.420
TIM HAYWARD: I think
now we're in lockdown,

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.193
we need to stop being
so damn judgmental.

00:06:07.193 --> 00:06:09.110
There's a lot to be said
for a good Neapolitan

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:11.680
pizza, which is very pure,
close to what we're making here.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:13.730
But - and if you tell
anybody I've said this,

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:15.680
I will come to your
house and I will kill you

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.960
and all those you love -
but I like ham and pineapple

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:19.810
on a pizza.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:21.050
Ooh, scandal.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:23.480
Sorry, my daughter's just
asked if she can be adopted.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:24.982
OK, let's do it.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:29.690
Oh, mate, that's really good.

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:30.440
I love your edges.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:31.515
Look, I'm just going
to lift mine up.

00:06:31.515 --> 00:06:32.185
Very nice.

00:06:32.185 --> 00:06:32.685
Let's cut.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:36.050
What you reckon?

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.435
That's pretty good.

00:06:37.435 --> 00:06:38.948
You should to be
able to think now

00:06:38.948 --> 00:06:40.990
of tweaks you could do to
that, because sourdough

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:43.583
goes so well with blue cheese,
or some slices of pear.

00:06:43.583 --> 00:06:45.250
You don't have to
stick to just Italian.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.530
Lahmacun is a Turkish flatbread.

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:50.050
Make the base that same way,
put olive oil all over it.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.370
Then you crumble seasoned
ground lamb over the top of it.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.150
Bit Of cumin, some
sumac, things like that

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:56.530
- bit of zaatar if you have it.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:00.030
When it comes out, you put dots
of cream cheese on top of it,

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:01.780
or it can work with
feta, if you want to.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:02.810
So there's so many
different ways

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:05.435
you can do it without having to
go through the whole routine of

00:07:05.435 --> 00:07:07.690
- I'm really bored of
making sourdough loaves now.

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.230
I've made 12 bigger
than my head.

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:09.940
I don't want to do anymore.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:12.565
The base is everything, and you
can taste the sour in the base.

00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:15.410
You can taste that that's
sourdough pizza for sure.

